<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Rajahnate of Cebu</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1565 CE (approx.) · capital Sugbu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Visayan port kingdom of Sugbu, where Magellan baptised Rajah Humabon in 1521 and Spain founded its first Philippine settlement in 1565.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> grew as a trading port on the east coast of Cebu Island, linked to Chinese, Siamese and other Asian traders by the 15th century; Chinese ceramics are common in its burials. A later tradition, the Aginid, attributes its founding to a prince called Sri Lumay, but this is not supported by contemporary sources.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> was ruled by a rajah over datus, freemen and dependants, and taxed visiting ships. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-pigafetta">Antonio Pigafetta</a>, who arrived with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a> in April 1521, described its trade and court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-humabon">Rajah Humabon</a> and hundreds of his people were baptised, but the alliance broke down after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapulapu">Lapulapu</a> of Mactan killed Magellan in battle on 27 April 1521.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</a> arrived in 1565 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-humabon">Humabon</a>'s successor Rajah Tupas submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> became the first Spanish city in the islands and the seat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a> until 1571. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magellans-cross">Magellan's Cross</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-nino-de-cebu">Santo Nino</a> image recall the first encounter.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rajahnate-of-cebu.jpg" alt="Rajahnate of Cebu" width="320" height="415"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sugbumap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1565 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sugbu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Humabon, Tupas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous Philippine religion, later Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cebuano</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Philippines</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sugbu, Cebu</dd></dl></aside></div>

